    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a> in 1523, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> stayed under one Oldenburg king for nearly three centuries.</li><li>A Lutheran state funded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">the Sound</a> Toll, it became an absolute monarchy in 1660 under the written King’s Law of 1665.</li><li>It lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1658, and defeat as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>’s ally forced it to cede <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> to Sweden in 1814.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1792 Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> became the first European state to ban the transatlantic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> by law, though the ban only took effect in 1803.</p></aside>
    </section>

