    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/margaret-i-of-denmark"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/margaret-i-of-denmark.jpg" alt="Margaret I of Denmark" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/margaret-i-of-denmark">Margaret I of Denmark</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Margaret_of_Denmark,_Norway_%26_Sweden_(1389)_effigy_2010_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacob Truedson Demitz assisted by Emil Eikner for Ristesson, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a>, the union of the three Scandinavian crowns formed in 1397 under Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-i-of-denmark">Margaret I</a>. In 1523 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> broke away under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-i-of-sweden">Gustav Vasa</a>, and in the same year the Danish nobility deposed the Union king Christian II and elected his uncle, Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-denmark">Frederick of Schleswig-Holstein</a>, as Frederick I; Norway accepted him in 1524. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and Norway thus remained under one king of the house of Oldenburg, together with Iceland, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a> and the Norse claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>, while the king also ruled the duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and Holstein. After Frederick’s death in 1533 a civil war, the Count’s Feud of 1534–1536, pitted supporters of the imprisoned Christian II, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, against Frederick’s son Christian III. Christian III’s victory brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Lutheran Reformation</a>: in 1536 the Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> were arrested and their lands confiscated by the crown, and a Lutheran church ordinance followed in 1537. Christian III’s accession charter of 1536 declared that Norway should no longer be a separate kingdom but a part of the Danish realm, a change made effective when the last Catholic archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Nidaros</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olav-engelbrektsson">Olav Engelbrektsson</a>, fled the country in 1537. Norway nonetheless kept its own laws and much of its own administration.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bergen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bergen.jpg" alt="Bergen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bergen_panorama_at_night_-_panoramio_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TomasEE, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The realm combined two very different lands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a> east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">the Sound</a> until 1658, was a fertile agricultural country of grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, dominated by noble estates worked by tenant farmers; from 1733 the <em>stavnsbånd</em> tied Danish peasant men to the estates where they were born, until the agrarian reforms of 1788 abolished it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> was a land of freehold and tenant farmers, fishermen and foresters, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, sawn in water-powered mills, was exported in large quantities to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and whose dried and salted fish from the north was traded through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> mines at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kongsberg">Kongsberg</a>, opened in 1623, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mines at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roros">Røros</a>, from 1644, added to royal revenue. In the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, of 1769, Denmark had roughly 800,000 inhabitants and Norway about 720,000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, the capital, grew into the largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. The crown drew a large income from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sound-toll">Sound Toll</a>, levied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Helsingør</a> on ships passing between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> and the Baltic. The Lutheran church, staffed by university-trained pastors, shaped education and daily life, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietism">Pietism</a> gained royal favour in the eighteenth century. Danish trading companies dealt in Asian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, textiles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>, much of it re-exported.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutter"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutter.jpg" alt="Battle of Lutter" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutter">Battle of Lutter</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht_bei_Lutter_am_Barenberge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Michael Caspar Lundorp, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> fought a long series of wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> for control of the Baltic and its approaches. The Northern Seven Years’ War of 1563–1570 ended in stalemate at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stettin-1570">Treaty of Stettin</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-war">Kalmar War</a> of 1611–1613 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-iv-of-denmark">Christian IV</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvsborg">Älvsborg</a> and forced Sweden to pay a heavy ransom for it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-knared">Treaty of Knäred</a>. Christian IV’s intervention in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years’ War</a> ended in defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-tserclaes-count-of-tilly">Tilly</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutter">Lutter am Barenberge</a> in 1626. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torstenson-war">Torstenson War</a> of 1643–1645 Swedish armies overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a>, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bromsebro-1645">Treaty of Brömsebro</a> Denmark-Norway ceded Gotland, Ösel and the Norwegian provinces of Jämtland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harjedalen">Härjedalen</a>. Worse followed in 1658, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> of Sweden marched his army across the frozen Belts and imposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a>, which took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halland">Halland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blekinge">Blekinge</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohuslan">Bohuslän</a>, Trøndelag and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornholm">Bornholm</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> survived a Swedish assault in 1659, and Trøndelag and Bornholm were returned in 1660. The navy was the realm’s main strength: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niels-juel">Niels Juel</a> defeated the Swedes at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-koge-bay">Battle of Køge Bay</a> in 1677, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-tordenskjold">Peter Tordenskjold</a> won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dynekilen">Dynekilen</a> in 1716 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>. A separate Norwegian army was organised from 1628, and fortresses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kronborg">Kronborg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akershus-fortress">Akershus</a> and Fredriksten guarded the realm.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kings-law"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kings-law.jpg" alt="King's Law" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kings-law">King's Law</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kongeloven_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rigsarkivet - Danish National Archives, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Until 1660 the king governed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigsradet">Council of the Realm</a>, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigsrad">Rigsråd</a></em>, drawn from the high nobility, and each new king signed an accession charter limiting his powers. The disasters of 1658–1660 changed this. At a meeting of the estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> in 1660, clergy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a>, resentful of noble privilege, joined Frederick III in making the monarchy hereditary, and in 1661 he became absolute ruler. The King’s Law, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kings-law">Kongeloven</a></em> of 1665, defined this absolutism in writing, the only European constitution of its kind. Government was reorganised into colleges, or departmental boards, staffed increasingly by commoners and German-speaking officials, and a new nobility of counts and barons was created. Christian V issued uniform law codes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danish-code">Danish Code</a> of 1683 and the Norwegian Code of 1687. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> was governed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> or <em>stattholder</em> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Christiania</a> and kept its own law and army. Absolutism allowed rapid reform but also abuse: in 1770–1772 the royal physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-friedrich-struensee">Johann Friedrich Struensee</a> ruled in the name of the mentally ill Christian VII, issuing hundreds of decrees, including freedom of the press, before he was overthrown and executed. Later reforming ministers abolished the <em>stavnsbånd</em> in 1788 and decreed in 1792 an end to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, effective from 1803.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/uraniborg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uraniborg.jpg" alt="Uraniborg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/uraniborg">Uraniborg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uraniborgskiss_45.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stanlekub, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> left a lasting imprint on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-europe">Northern Europe</a>. Its Lutheran church and absolute monarchy shaped the political culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and Norway, and the Danish written language became the language of administration and literature in Norway, the basis of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bokmal">Bokmål</a> standard. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludvig-holberg">Ludvig Holberg</a>, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>, wrote comedies and histories that founded modern Danish-Norwegian literature, and the astronomer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tycho-brahe">Tycho Brahe</a>, supported by Frederick II at his observatory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uraniborg">Uraniborg</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ven-island">Hven</a>, made the most precise naked-eye observations of his age; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ole-romer">Ole Rømer</a> first measured the speed of light in 1676. The realm also built a modest overseas empire: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharangambadi">Tranquebar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1620, forts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, and St Thomas, St John and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">St Croix</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations relied on enslaved Africans. The Danish decree of 1792 made Denmark-Norway the first European state to ban the transatlantic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> by law. The missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-egede">Hans Egede</a> began the Danish recolonisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> in 1721, the origin of the present link between Denmark and Greenland. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-iv-of-denmark">Christian IV</a>’s building projects, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosenborg-castle">Rosenborg</a>, the Round Tower and the new Norwegian capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Christiania</a>, founded in 1624, still mark the cities of both countries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-copenhagen-1801"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-copenhagen-1801.jpg" alt="Battle of Copenhagen (1801)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-copenhagen-1801">Battle of Copenhagen (1801)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Battle_of_Copenhagen,_2_April_1801_RMG_BHC0529.tiff" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nicholas Pocock, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> prospered as a neutral trading nation during the wars of the late eighteenth century, but its neutrality brought conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-armed-neutrality">League of Armed Neutrality</a>, a British fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyde-parker">Hyde Parker</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Horatio Nelson</a> defeated the Danish fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-copenhagen-1801">Battle of Copenhagen</a> in 1801. In 1807, fearing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> would seize the Danish navy, Britain bombarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> and carried off the fleet, driving Denmark-Norway into alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> cut Norway off from Danish grain and caused famine in 1808–1809, and the state went bankrupt in 1813. After Napoleon’s defeat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-vi-of-denmark">Frederick VI</a> was forced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kiel">Treaty of Kiel</a> in January 1814 to cede Norway to the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, while Denmark kept Iceland, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>, which had been Norwegian dependencies. Norwegians refused to accept the transfer: an assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eidsvoll">Eidsvoll</a> adopted a constitution on 17 May 1814 and elected the Danish prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-viii-of-denmark">Christian Frederick</a> as king of an independent Kingdom of Norway. After a brief war with Sweden, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-of-moss">Convention of Moss</a> led to a personal union, Sweden-Norway, in November 1814. The Norwegian constitution survived, and the separation from Denmark became the foundation of modern Norway, independent in 1905.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> joined two Scandinavian kingdoms under the Oldenburg kings for nearly three centuries, building a Lutheran absolute monarchy, a strong navy and a small overseas empire while losing the struggle for the Baltic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and its break-up in 1814 shaped modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, Norway, Iceland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>.</em></p>

